      "updated": 1780914105851,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 2b: Forking the identical copies\n\n-> fork() creates a child process that is exactly same as the parent process(creates a copy of pcb and memory layout)\n-> the child process and the parent process start executing from the moment the fork() call returns, for child it returns 0,\n    for parent it returns the pid of child process.\n-> since the memory layout is a copy, the logical addresses in the child process are exactly same, but they are different entities in \n    the physical memory.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 2b: Forking the identical copies\n\n-> fork() creates a child process that is exactly same as the parent process(creates a copy of pcb and memory layout)\n-> the child process and the parent process start executing from the moment the fork() call returns, for child it returns 0,\n    for parent it returns the pid of child process.\n-> since the memory layout is a copy, the logical addresses in the child process are exactly same, but they are different entities in \n    the physical memory.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
