      "updated": 1780922253881,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 4: Exec\n\n-> exec(\"inputfile.out)\n-> the process that executes this sys call, will have it's main-memory contents reset and the\n    new program's code and data will be loaded.\n-> The pcb however needs to retain pid, memory bounds, process relationships, cpu metrics and open file descriptors\n\nUsing Fork and Exec, this is how unix-shell works:\n\n        \n    while(true) {\n        program = input(\"Program's name\");\n\n        if(search(program)) {\n            if(fork() == 0) {\n                exec(program)\n            }\n            else {\n                wait()\n            }\n        }\n        else {\n            output(\"No executable found\");\n        }\n    }\n    \n-> System Calls: Are entry points to code in kernel that perform critical actions.  \n-> User programs are not allowed to perform these critical actions manually, as they can hijack the resources.\n-> So, OS provide these entry points to request an action to be performed.\n-> Types of System Calls:\n    Process Control\n    File Manipulation\n    Device Manipulation\n    Information Maintainence\n    Communication\n    Protection\n\n-> The CPU operates in two modes, User Mode and Kernel Mode.\n-> The CPU after decoding every instruction, checks, if the instruction is a privileged instruction. If yes, then it checks the \"mode bit\" on the chip.\n-> So, when a syscall is executed, the cpu uses a mechanism called \"Trap\" to change the mode bit to 0 and then the kernel code will be executed. And mode bit will be set to 1.\n-> If a privileged instruction is sent to cpu and mode bit is not 0, cpu will trigger a hardware exception called a \"Privileged Instruction Violation\" (or General Protection Fault). \n    The CPU forces the mode bit to 0 and jumps to the OS exception handler, which promptly kills your application.\n-> The API's provided by POSIX are not actual syscalls, rather they wrap the real syscalls and might provide additional functionality and some might not call actual syscalls at all.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 4: Exec\n\n-> exec(\"inputfile.out)\n-> the process that executes this sys call, will have it's main-memory contents reset and the\n    new program's code and data will be loaded.\n-> The pcb however needs to retain pid, memory bounds, process relationships, cpu metrics and open file descriptors\n\nUsing Fork and Exec, this is how unix-shell works:\n\n        \n    while(true) {\n        program = input(\"Program's name\");\n\n        if(search(program)) {\n            if(fork() == 0) {\n                exec(program)\n            }\n            else {\n                wait()\n            }\n        }\n        else {\n            output(\"No executable found\");\n        }\n    }\n    \n-> System Calls: Are entry points to code in kernel that perform critical actions.  \n-> User programs are not allowed to perform these critical actions manually, as they can hijack the resources.\n-> So, OS provide these entry points to request an action to be performed.\n-> Types of System Calls:\n    Process Control\n    File Manipulation\n    Device Manipulation\n    Information Maintainence\n    Communication\n    Protection\n\n-> The CPU operates in two modes, User Mode and Kernel Mode.\n-> The CPU after decoding every instruction, checks, if the instruction is a privileged instruction. If yes, then it checks the \"mode bit\" on the chip.\n-> So, when a syscall is executed, the cpu uses a mechanism called \"Trap\" to change the mode bit to 0 and then the kernel code will be executed. And mode bit will be set to 1.\n-> If a privileged instruction is sent to cpu and mode bit is not 0, cpu will trigger a hardware exception called a \"Privileged Instruction Violation\" (or General Protection Fault). \n    The CPU forces the mode bit to 0 and jumps to the OS exception handler, which promptly kills your application.\n-> The API's provided by POSIX are not actual syscalls, rather they wrap the real syscalls and might provide additional functionality and some might not call actual syscalls at all.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "toHlc5cQER-_U-rjzrUvv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 962.5914627225033,
      "y": -8702.30095714498,
      "width": 1264.4189453125,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
