      "updated": 1780990424243,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 7: Thread Mapping Architecture\n\n-> Though it is common to say that OS runs processes and manages them, it is not the \"Process\" that is the execution unit that runs,\n    It is actually the \"Thread\".\n-> Process is like super-structure that maintains the executions units called \"Threads\"\n\nMany-To-One Mapping:\n\n-> this is when you create multiple user level threads per a process.\n-> the advantages and disadvantages are listed in lecture 6 under user-level threads.\n\nOne-To-One Mapping:\n\n-> this is default. Creating a new process automatically creates a new Kernel-Level Thread. So One KLT per Program/User-Level Thread\n-> Same advantages and disadvantages listed in lecture 6 under Kernel-Level threads.\n\nMany-To-Many Mapping:\n\n-> Combines best of both M:O and O:O\n-> It multiplexes many user-level threads onto smaller or same number of KLTs.\n-> it requires a precise co-operation b/w application runtime and kernel.\n-> the user can spawn any number of user-level threads and the run time carefully maps these to the KLTs.\n-> There by providing parallelism to many user-level threads at once.\n-> When a ULT mapped to one of the KLTs issues an I/O, the run-tiime maps the remaining ULTs onto different KLTs to continue \n   execution.\n-> this is complex to build and The Go-routines are built on top of this architecture.\n\nContext Switch:\n\n-> It is when a running process on a cpu core is stopped and another process is scheduled to run on the same cpu core.\n-> Both hardware and Software(OS) co-ordinate to do this.\n\nFlow of Context Switch:\n\n1. Interrupt Fires:\n    - Process P1 is running in User Mode. A hardware interrupt occurs (e.g., a timer slice expiration or an I/O device signaling).\n\n2. Hardware State Save (Atomic Circuit Sequence):\n    - The CPU hardware instantly switches the Mode Bit to Kernel Mode (`0`) and automatically shifts the physical Stack Pointer (SP) to point to P1s Kernel Stack. \n    - It pushes the following core values onto $P_1$'s Kernel Stack for safekeeping:\n        * Saved User Program Counter (User PC)\n        * Saved Processor Flags\n        * Saved User Stack Pointer (User SP)\n\n3. Vector Table Lookup\n    - The CPU hardware reads the Interrupt Vector Table (IVT) in RAM and overwrites the physical Program Counter (PC) register with the memory address of the corresponding **Interrupt Handler** routine.\n\n4. Software State Save\n    - The Interrupt Handler code begins executing in Kernel Mode. It runs a sequence of assembly instructions (`PUSH`) to save all **remaining general-purpose registers** of P1 onto P1's Kernel Stack. \n    - At this point, P1's state is completely frozen and preserved.\n\n5. Decision Making (The Scheduler)\n    - The Interrupt Handler invokes the kernel's **Scheduler** function module. The Scheduler checks the Ready Queue data structure, runs its configured scheduling algorithm (e.g., Round Robin)\n      to select **Process P2** to run next, and hands execution control over to the **Dispatcher** routine.\n\n6. The Stack Pointer Swap\n    - The Dispatcher reads P2's Process Control Block (PCB) to find where P2's Kernel Stack was previously paused. It executes a critical line of assembly that updates the physical CPU Stack Pointer (SP) register \n      to point directly to **P2's Kernel Stack**. \n    - Architectural Milestone: The context has now officially shifted from P1 to P2.\n\n7. Partial Context Restore\n    - Operating entirely on P2's Kernel Stack, the Dispatcher executes assembly `POP` instructions to restore P2's **general-purpose registers** back into the physical hardware channels.\n\n8. The IRET Leap (Atomic Hardware Return)\n    - The Dispatcher executes the `IRET` (Interrupt Return)** instruction. The CPU hardware control unit takes over and pops the final remaining values off the top of P2's Kernel Stack in a single atomic cycle:\n      * Pops the saved flags back into the status register.\n      * Pops the saved User PC into the physical Program Counter register.\n      * Pops the saved User SP back into the physical Stack Pointer (SP) register (shifting focus completely back to User Space memory layout).\n      * Toggles the hardware **Mode Bit** from Kernel Mode (`0`) back to User Mode (`1`).\n\n9. Execution Resumes\n    - Process P2 starts executing its next sequential application instruction smoothly, completely unaware that it was ever paused or context-switched!",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 7: Thread Mapping Architecture\n\n-> Though it is common to say that OS runs processes and manages them, it is not the \"Process\" that is the execution unit that runs,\n    It is actually the \"Thread\".\n-> Process is like super-structure that maintains the executions units called \"Threads\"\n\nMany-To-One Mapping:\n\n-> this is when you create multiple user level threads per a process.\n-> the advantages and disadvantages are listed in lecture 6 under user-level threads.\n\nOne-To-One Mapping:\n\n-> this is default. Creating a new process automatically creates a new Kernel-Level Thread. So One KLT per Program/User-Level Thread\n-> Same advantages and disadvantages listed in lecture 6 under Kernel-Level threads.\n\nMany-To-Many Mapping:\n\n-> Combines best of both M:O and O:O\n-> It multiplexes many user-level threads onto smaller or same number of KLTs.\n-> it requires a precise co-operation b/w application runtime and kernel.\n-> the user can spawn any number of user-level threads and the run time carefully maps these to the KLTs.\n-> There by providing parallelism to many user-level threads at once.\n-> When a ULT mapped to one of the KLTs issues an I/O, the run-tiime maps the remaining ULTs onto different KLTs to continue \n   execution.\n-> this is complex to build and The Go-routines are built on top of this architecture.\n\nContext Switch:\n\n-> It is when a running process on a cpu core is stopped and another process is scheduled to run on the same cpu core.\n-> Both hardware and Software(OS) co-ordinate to do this.\n\nFlow of Context Switch:\n\n1. Interrupt Fires:\n    - Process P1 is running in User Mode. A hardware interrupt occurs (e.g., a timer slice expiration or an I/O device signaling).\n\n2. Hardware State Save (Atomic Circuit Sequence):\n    - The CPU hardware instantly switches the Mode Bit to Kernel Mode (`0`) and automatically shifts the physical Stack Pointer (SP) to point to P1s Kernel Stack. \n    - It pushes the following core values onto $P_1$'s Kernel Stack for safekeeping:\n        * Saved User Program Counter (User PC)\n        * Saved Processor Flags\n        * Saved User Stack Pointer (User SP)\n\n3. Vector Table Lookup\n    - The CPU hardware reads the Interrupt Vector Table (IVT) in RAM and overwrites the physical Program Counter (PC) register with the memory address of the corresponding **Interrupt Handler** routine.\n\n4. Software State Save\n    - The Interrupt Handler code begins executing in Kernel Mode. It runs a sequence of assembly instructions (`PUSH`) to save all **remaining general-purpose registers** of P1 onto P1's Kernel Stack. \n    - At this point, P1's state is completely frozen and preserved.\n\n5. Decision Making (The Scheduler)\n    - The Interrupt Handler invokes the kernel's **Scheduler** function module. The Scheduler checks the Ready Queue data structure, runs its configured scheduling algorithm (e.g., Round Robin)\n      to select **Process P2** to run next, and hands execution control over to the **Dispatcher** routine.\n\n6. The Stack Pointer Swap\n    - The Dispatcher reads P2's Process Control Block (PCB) to find where P2's Kernel Stack was previously paused. It executes a critical line of assembly that updates the physical CPU Stack Pointer (SP) register \n      to point directly to **P2's Kernel Stack**. \n    - Architectural Milestone: The context has now officially shifted from P1 to P2.\n\n7. Partial Context Restore\n    - Operating entirely on P2's Kernel Stack, the Dispatcher executes assembly `POP` instructions to restore P2's **general-purpose registers** back into the physical hardware channels.\n\n8. The IRET Leap (Atomic Hardware Return)\n    - The Dispatcher executes the `IRET` (Interrupt Return)** instruction. The CPU hardware control unit takes over and pops the final remaining values off the top of P2's Kernel Stack in a single atomic cycle:\n      * Pops the saved flags back into the status register.\n      * Pops the saved User PC into the physical Program Counter register.\n      * Pops the saved User SP back into the physical Stack Pointer (SP) register (shifting focus completely back to User Space memory layout).\n      * Toggles the hardware **Mode Bit** from Kernel Mode (`0`) back to User Mode (`1`).\n\n9. Execution Resumes\n    - Process P2 starts executing its next sequential application instruction smoothly, completely unaware that it was ever paused or context-switched!",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "An8Pu5JIjI8JTeMqX5td4",
