      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 6:\n\nUser-Level Threads: \n\n-> The kernel doesn't know about the existence of User-Level Threads.\n-> it sees the collection of user-threads of a specific process as a single process.\n-> They are fast to spawn and kill.\n-> But since they are not seen by the kernel, they are not assigned to available cpus to run in parallel\n-> They are made using \"function calls\"\n-> A user-space thread scheduler schedules the threads in a process, in the time that the process is run on cpu by OS's Scheduler.\n-> So, if one of threads accesses an I/0, the cpu will consider while process is accessing an I/0 and removes the process from CPU.\n\nKernel-Level Threads:\n\n-> The Kernel knows about them and sees them as individual threads.\n-> So, they take advantage of available cpus.\n-> But they increase overhead and stress the whole system performance, as they need to monitored at kernel-level.\n-> They are made using \"system calls\"",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 6:\n\nUser-Level Threads: \n\n-> The kernel doesn't know about the existence of User-Level Threads.\n-> it sees the collection of user-threads of a specific process as a single process.\n-> They are fast to spawn and kill.\n-> But since they are not seen by the kernel, they are not assigned to available cpus to run in parallel\n-> They are made using \"function calls\"\n-> A user-space thread scheduler schedules the threads in a process, in the time that the process is run on cpu by OS's Scheduler.\n-> So, if one of threads accesses an I/0, the cpu will consider while process is accessing an I/0 and removes the process from CPU.\n\nKernel-Level Threads:\n\n-> The Kernel knows about them and sees them as individual threads.\n-> So, they take advantage of available cpus.\n-> But they increase overhead and stress the whole system performance, as they need to monitored at kernel-level.\n-> They are made using \"system calls\"",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
