    {
      "id": "3zvG6ZxLGEM1tPN5rJGOd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1025.5093187711375,
      "y": -4988.72911907022,
      "width": 1666.39892578125,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b59",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 193118889,
      "version": 1582,
      "versionNonce": 1155113927,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781003003694,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Types of Schedulers:\n\nLong-Term Scheduler: \n    - Controls the degree of multi-programming. i.e. number of processes residing in the memory at once.\n    - Controls the transition New->Ready and New->Ready Suspend. Dictates which programs are allowed to enter the system from disk to become active processes.\n    - In traditional desktops, this is not being used. It has it's importance in Real-time OS.\n\nMedium-Term Scheduler:\n    - It acts as the RAM optimiser. When the RAM is running out of space, it takes some of the processes in memory and places them in the hard disk. i.e. suspends them.\n    - It is also responsible for bringing the suspended processes back into the memory.\n    - It governs the transitions: Suspend Ready to Ready, Blocked to Suspend Blocked, Suspend Blocked to Blocked and Suspend Ready to Ready.\n\nShort-Term Scheduler:\n    - It decides which memory-resident process gets immediate execution time on a CPU core.\n    - Governs Ready to Running transition.\n    - Implements algorithms like round-robin, SRTF, Priority Scheduling etc. to empahsize specific metrics.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Types of Schedulers:\n\nLong-Term Scheduler: \n    - Controls the degree of multi-programming. i.e. number of processes residing in the memory at once.\n    - Controls the transition New->Ready and New->Ready Suspend. Dictates which programs are allowed to enter the system from disk to become active processes.\n    - In traditional desktops, this is not being used. It has it's importance in Real-time OS.\n\nMedium-Term Scheduler:\n    - It acts as the RAM optimiser. When the RAM is running out of space, it takes some of the processes in memory and places them in the hard disk. i.e. suspends them.\n    - It is also responsible for bringing the suspended processes back into the memory.\n    - It governs the transitions: Suspend Ready to Ready, Blocked to Suspend Blocked, Suspend Blocked to Blocked and Suspend Ready to Ready.\n\nShort-Term Scheduler:\n    - It decides which memory-resident process gets immediate execution time on a CPU core.\n    - Governs Ready to Running transition.\n    - Implements algorithms like round-robin, SRTF, Priority Scheduling etc. to empahsize specific metrics.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "n4MT_VSDY8fWqRDG9hbnJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1009.3465841822533,
      "y": -4462.072236892648,
      "width": 1627.91845703125,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 149645801,
      "version": 869,
      "versionNonce": 1546112169,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781005065839,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 9 : Scheduling Algorithms | FCFS\n\nCriteria to gauge the performance of Scheduling Algorithms:\n    - CPU Utilization\n    - Throughput\n    - Turnaround time\n    - Waiting time\n    - Response time\n\nArrival Time: The time at which the process enters the ready queue in main-memory for the first time\n\nFCFS (First Come First Serve):\n    - The process which arrives first runs until it completes the execution.\n    - The process won't release cpu no matter what until it completes the execution.\n    - For every process: Response Time = Waiting Time\n    - If long processes arrive first, the short processes that arrive later would have long response and waiting and turnaround times. This is due to \"Convoy Effect\". ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 9 : Scheduling Algorithms | FCFS\n\nCriteria to gauge the performance of Scheduling Algorithms:\n    - CPU Utilization\n    - Throughput\n    - Turnaround time\n    - Waiting time\n    - Response time\n\nArrival Time: The time at which the process enters the ready queue in main-memory for the first time\n\nFCFS (First Come First Serve):\n    - The process which arrives first runs until it completes the execution.\n    - The process won't release cpu no matter what until it completes the execution.\n    - For every process: Response Time = Waiting Time\n    - If long processes arrive first, the short processes that arrive later would have long response and waiting and turnaround times. This is due to \"Convoy Effect\". ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "7N0Fd-qxRZwAYW5dW0_NG",
