      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 10 : Shortest Job First (SJF)\n\n-> In FCFS, there is a convoy effect as all the other processes wait for the one big process to get off the CPU.\n    This effect results in lower CPU and Device utilisation than might be possible if the shorter processes were allowed to go first.\n-> Moving a short process before a long one decreases the waiting time of short process more than it increases\n    the waiting time of long processes. Consequently, The avg Waiting time decreases.\n-> So, SJF schedules the shortest job/process first\n\n-> But, SJF has to predict the run times of the processes in order to schedule them.\n-> One of the ways, it does this is by taking avg of actual run times(tn)\n-> Predict Time of Process(Pn) which will run for the nth time, \n\n        Sn = (t1 + t2 + ... + tn-1) / n-1\n        Sn = ((t1 + t2 + ... + tn-2) / n-1) + (tn-1 / n-1)\n        Sn = (n-2)Sn-1 / (n-1) + (tn-1 / n-1)\n\n-> This is called \"Simple Moving Average\" and gives equal weights to all burst times, by dynamically changing the weights after each run.\n\n-> Another way is to fix the weight.\n\n    Sn = Sn =  αtn-1 + (1-α)Sn-1,   0 <= α <= 1\n\n-> This is called \"Exponential Soothing\" Formula, we can emphasize either recent run times or previous run times based on the value of α.\n\n-> SJF can be either preemptive or non-preemptive. If a new process at the ready queue has shorter burst time than the \"remaining\" burst time of the \n    currently running process, then a preemptive scheduler would preempt the currently running process, whereas non-preemptive will allow the current\n    process to finish it's cpu burst.\n\n-> The Preemptive SJF scheduling is called \"Shortest-remaining-time-first\" Scheduling.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 10 : Shortest Job First (SJF)\n\n-> In FCFS, there is a convoy effect as all the other processes wait for the one big process to get off the CPU.\n    This effect results in lower CPU and Device utilisation than might be possible if the shorter processes were allowed to go first.\n-> Moving a short process before a long one decreases the waiting time of short process more than it increases\n    the waiting time of long processes. Consequently, The avg Waiting time decreases.\n-> So, SJF schedules the shortest job/process first\n\n-> But, SJF has to predict the run times of the processes in order to schedule them.\n-> One of the ways, it does this is by taking avg of actual run times(tn)\n-> Predict Time of Process(Pn) which will run for the nth time, \n\n        Sn = (t1 + t2 + ... + tn-1) / n-1\n        Sn = ((t1 + t2 + ... + tn-2) / n-1) + (tn-1 / n-1)\n        Sn = (n-2)Sn-1 / (n-1) + (tn-1 / n-1)\n\n-> This is called \"Simple Moving Average\" and gives equal weights to all burst times, by dynamically changing the weights after each run.\n\n-> Another way is to fix the weight.\n\n    Sn = Sn =  αtn-1 + (1-α)Sn-1,   0 <= α <= 1\n\n-> This is called \"Exponential Soothing\" Formula, we can emphasize either recent run times or previous run times based on the value of α.\n\n-> SJF can be either preemptive or non-preemptive. If a new process at the ready queue has shorter burst time than the \"remaining\" burst time of the \n    currently running process, then a preemptive scheduler would preempt the currently running process, whereas non-preemptive will allow the current\n    process to finish it's cpu burst.\n\n-> The Preemptive SJF scheduling is called \"Shortest-remaining-time-first\" Scheduling.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "39HtqQ4ryQyeRUZwxcKrc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1005.3239847380128,
      "y": -3171.2683195968148,
      "width": 1396.9986572265625,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1322400113,
      "version": 1362,
      "versionNonce": 810595185,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781077483000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 11 : Round Robin\n\n-> RR scheduling is Preemptive and designed for time-sharing\n-> It defines a time quantum or time slice: a fixed interval of time.\n-> Unless there is only one process in the ready queue, it never runs a process for more\n    than one quantum time.\n-> This is implemented with the help of hardware timer. the timer will raise an interrupt at\n    completion of every quantum time and a context switch happens to run next process \n    in the ready queue. The choice of picking the next process can be done using any algorithm.\n    but, by default, we assume it is fcfs.\n\nAdvantages:\n-> Low response time and good interactivity\n-> Fair allocation of cpu across processes\n-> Low average waiting time when job lengths vary widely\n\nDisadvantages:\n-> Poor average waiting time when jobs have similar lengths(can be worse than FCFS), i.e. waiting time and TAT are similar or worse than FCFS\n-> Performance depends on length of time slice:\n    - too high: degenerate to FCFS\n    - too small: too many context switches",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 11 : Round Robin\n\n-> RR scheduling is Preemptive and designed for time-sharing\n-> It defines a time quantum or time slice: a fixed interval of time.\n-> Unless there is only one process in the ready queue, it never runs a process for more\n    than one quantum time.\n-> This is implemented with the help of hardware timer. the timer will raise an interrupt at\n    completion of every quantum time and a context switch happens to run next process \n    in the ready queue. The choice of picking the next process can be done using any algorithm.\n    but, by default, we assume it is fcfs.\n\nAdvantages:\n-> Low response time and good interactivity\n-> Fair allocation of cpu across processes\n-> Low average waiting time when job lengths vary widely\n\nDisadvantages:\n-> Poor average waiting time when jobs have similar lengths(can be worse than FCFS), i.e. waiting time and TAT are similar or worse than FCFS\n-> Performance depends on length of time slice:\n    - too high: degenerate to FCFS\n    - too small: too many context switches",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "uqAQHQz_FWUQVBvJ8KgEz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 982.1936812245065,
      "y": -2579.5656429909477,
      "width": 1670.69873046875,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 787228241,
      "version": 878,
      "versionNonce": 1173153215,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781093658857,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 12 : SRTF\n\n-> When a process releases cpu to perform i/o, it's remaining time is updated. The process\n    with least remaining time will be scheduled next.\n\n-> Also, whenever a new process is added to the ready queue, if it's burst time is less than the current processes remaining time, the new process\n    will be scheduled and current process will be queued.\n\n-> If remaining times are equal, DO NOT preempt (favor the currently running process). If a tie occurs inside the ready queue, break via FCFS (lowest Arrival Time / PID).\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 12 : SRTF\n\n-> When a process releases cpu to perform i/o, it's remaining time is updated. The process\n    with least remaining time will be scheduled next.\n\n-> Also, whenever a new process is added to the ready queue, if it's burst time is less than the current processes remaining time, the new process\n    will be scheduled and current process will be queued.\n\n-> If remaining times are equal, DO NOT preempt (favor the currently running process). If a tie occurs inside the ready queue, break via FCFS (lowest Arrival Time / PID).\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "T54OYUtHadDz8g8Jb8OJz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 972.5569196572899,
      "y": -2286.4388548265906,
      "width": 1727.238525390625,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5P",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 241102271,
      "version": 1607,
      "versionNonce": 1233517969,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781093528299,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 13 : Priority Scheduling\n\n-> Priority Scheduling can be both Preemptive and non-preemptive.\n\nStatic Priority: Priority is assigned at birth and remains fixed.\nDynamic Priority: Priority changes dynamically over time. (Primary tool for fixing \"Starvation\" via \"Aging\" — increasing priority of long-waiting jobs).\n\n->For Both Static And Dynamic:\n    - If Non-Preemptive: Running process keeps CPU until it terminates or blocks for I/O.\n    - If Preemptive: An unblocking process or a newly arriving process with higher priority will instantly kick the current process off the CPU.\n\n\nIN GATE QUESTIONS, ALWAYS ASSUME THAT THE CPU WILL BE RELEASED WHEN A PROCESS BLOCKS FOR AN I/O UNLESS SOMETHING IS MENTIONED IN QUESTION.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 13 : Priority Scheduling\n\n-> Priority Scheduling can be both Preemptive and non-preemptive.\n\nStatic Priority: Priority is assigned at birth and remains fixed.\nDynamic Priority: Priority changes dynamically over time. (Primary tool for fixing \"Starvation\" via \"Aging\" — increasing priority of long-waiting jobs).\n\n->For Both Static And Dynamic:\n    - If Non-Preemptive: Running process keeps CPU until it terminates or blocks for I/O.\n    - If Preemptive: An unblocking process or a newly arriving process with higher priority will instantly kick the current process off the CPU.\n\n\nIN GATE QUESTIONS, ALWAYS ASSUME THAT THE CPU WILL BE RELEASED WHEN A PROCESS BLOCKS FOR AN I/O UNLESS SOMETHING IS MENTIONED IN QUESTION.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
