      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "u2FaSF9utJuvc45ECG-Rm",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1781413350881,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 24 : Questions on Base Bound and Segmentation\n\nFor negative direction growth:\n-> Think, [base - bound, base)\n-> Then Physical Address = Base + (offset - Max Size in offset bits)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 24 : Questions on Base Bound and Segmentation\n\nFor negative direction growth:\n-> Think, [base - bound, base)\n-> Then Physical Address = Base + (offset - Max Size in offset bits)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wLlSHh5Wh9w8ab1j923ZD",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 977.0342985990139,
      "y": 2931.3018558882773,
      "width": 1008.839111328125,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 353969585,
      "version": 1242,
      "versionNonce": 2008220671,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781259539978,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 25: Introduction to Paging\n\n-> Divide content into fixed-sized pages.\n-> Each page has a page number.\n-> Locate content in a page by an offset.\n-> There is a table that tells, which content is in which page\n-> Solves external fragmentation by using fixed and equal size page in both logical and physical memory.\n    Pages are called \"Frames\" in Physical memory.\n\n-> In addition to PPN, we can track additional info per entry in the page table.\n-> Protection Bit, Dirty Bit, Valid Bit, Present Bit, Referenced bit\n\n-> A Typical System has page size = 4kb and 32-bit Logical Address\n-> that gives 2^32/2^12 = 2^20 pages.\n-> Page table has 2^20 entries, each entry is 4 Bytes long.\n-> That gives, 2^20 x 4 = 4MB of Page table per EACH PROCESS.\n-> Processes actual size can be very small, but since page table maps every virtual page in the Logical\n    Address space they are sometimes exponentially larger than the actual process size.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 25: Introduction to Paging\n\n-> Divide content into fixed-sized pages.\n-> Each page has a page number.\n-> Locate content in a page by an offset.\n-> There is a table that tells, which content is in which page\n-> Solves external fragmentation by using fixed and equal size page in both logical and physical memory.\n    Pages are called \"Frames\" in Physical memory.\n\n-> In addition to PPN, we can track additional info per entry in the page table.\n-> Protection Bit, Dirty Bit, Valid Bit, Present Bit, Referenced bit\n\n-> A Typical System has page size = 4kb and 32-bit Logical Address\n-> that gives 2^32/2^12 = 2^20 pages.\n-> Page table has 2^20 entries, each entry is 4 Bytes long.\n-> That gives, 2^20 x 4 = 4MB of Page table per EACH PROCESS.\n-> Processes actual size can be very small, but since page table maps every virtual page in the Logical\n    Address space they are sometimes exponentially larger than the actual process size.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
