      "text": "Lecture 26 : Single level Paging questions",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 26 : Single level Paging questions",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "VO019xje1LgNDforxF1Ly",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 978.9963161967606,
      "y": 3511.070998404717,
      "width": 1470.4783935546875,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1891927679,
      "version": 2242,
      "versionNonce": 392191448,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781507741462,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 27, 28, 29 : Multi-Level Paging\n\n-> In a flat page table, To determine the Physical Page mapped to a specific virtual page, we need to find the PTE of the virtual page,\n    We do this by the formula (base of page table + (vpn * size of pte)). This is what allows fast lookups and this depends on the virtual\n    address structure. \n-> So, It is absolutely necessary to maintain the mapping of entire virtual address space with physical address space.\n-> But, It is possible to minimise the actual allocated space to store \"the mappings\". This is how it is done:\n\n-> We divide the flat table into \"chunks\" of \"page size\" in bytes. We allocate space to this chunk only if it contains atleast one PTE that is\n    mapped to physical frame.\n-> This way we don't break the technique of mapping, but reduce the allocated space for the mappings.\n\n-> Now, another level of page tables, often called \"page directory\" will have entries that map virtual addresses to one of the \"chunks\" or \n    \"pages of flat page table\".\n\n-> Generally, the page directory will also be divided into chunks of page size, we increase levels by dividing the page tables at each level into \"page\"\n    sized chunks, until every level has only page sized chunks of page tables.\n\n-> Let's say we have 32-bit VA and PA, and page size of 4KB and PTE size of 4 bytes. Then the VA is divided into these sections:\n\n    MSB <- 10 bits for First Level <-> 10 bits for Second Level <-> 12 bits for Offset in Frame -> LSB\n\n-> Operating Systems enforce this \"Fit Page Tables int Pages\" rule for efficiency and ease of access, But it is entirely possible to have variable sized\n    \"chunks\" at each level",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 27, 28, 29 : Multi-Level Paging\n\n-> In a flat page table, To determine the Physical Page mapped to a specific virtual page, we need to find the PTE of the virtual page,\n    We do this by the formula (base of page table + (vpn * size of pte)). This is what allows fast lookups and this depends on the virtual\n    address structure. \n-> So, It is absolutely necessary to maintain the mapping of entire virtual address space with physical address space.\n-> But, It is possible to minimise the actual allocated space to store \"the mappings\". This is how it is done:\n\n-> We divide the flat table into \"chunks\" of \"page size\" in bytes. We allocate space to this chunk only if it contains atleast one PTE that is\n    mapped to physical frame.\n-> This way we don't break the technique of mapping, but reduce the allocated space for the mappings.\n\n-> Now, another level of page tables, often called \"page directory\" will have entries that map virtual addresses to one of the \"chunks\" or \n    \"pages of flat page table\".\n\n-> Generally, the page directory will also be divided into chunks of page size, we increase levels by dividing the page tables at each level into \"page\"\n    sized chunks, until every level has only page sized chunks of page tables.\n\n-> Let's say we have 32-bit VA and PA, and page size of 4KB and PTE size of 4 bytes. Then the VA is divided into these sections:\n\n    MSB <- 10 bits for First Level <-> 10 bits for Second Level <-> 12 bits for Offset in Frame -> LSB\n\n-> Operating Systems enforce this \"Fit Page Tables int Pages\" rule for efficiency and ease of access, But it is entirely possible to have variable sized\n    \"chunks\" at each level",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ADl8X6mAFG5haCxFNhpxt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 979.486948538841,
      "y": 4172.102420540614,
      "width": 1585.898681640625,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5v",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 229921069,
      "version": 1436,
      "versionNonce": 15989928,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781520534437,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 30 : Memory Management Summary So Far, Hashed Page Table and Inverted Page Table\n\nHashed Page Table: \n-> Just store only allocated Page entries in a hash table, the virtual page number is used as key for hashing.\n\nInverted Page Table:\n-> There is only one Page Table that indexes Physical Frames. Each PTE has PID, VPN.\n-> Look ups take O(N) time and we match PID and VPN to identify the virtual page mapped to a specific Physical Frame.\n-> To make the look-ups fast, we can have a separate hash table, where PID + VPN is the key and each entry stores the physical address of PTE entry of IPT.\n    It also stores PID and VPN in the chain to identify the Physical Frame.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 30 : Memory Management Summary So Far, Hashed Page Table and Inverted Page Table\n\nHashed Page Table: \n-> Just store only allocated Page entries in a hash table, the virtual page number is used as key for hashing.\n\nInverted Page Table:\n-> There is only one Page Table that indexes Physical Frames. Each PTE has PID, VPN.\n-> Look ups take O(N) time and we match PID and VPN to identify the virtual page mapped to a specific Physical Frame.\n-> To make the look-ups fast, we can have a separate hash table, where PID + VPN is the key and each entry stores the physical address of PTE entry of IPT.\n    It also stores PID and VPN in the chain to identify the Physical Frame.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HeglZLD9G2mDVmrueb6EG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 982.8981969960578,
      "y": 4483.58683807429,
      "width": 1309.8388671875,
      "height": 275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b62",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1080992472,
      "version": 1048,
      "versionNonce": 1059684824,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781523128362,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 31: Segmentation with Paging\n\n-> Segmentation solves Internal Fragmentation, Paging solves external fragmentation. Combine Both.\n-> Each process has \"segment\" number of page tables. One per each segment.\n-> The base and bound registers will be there per each segment. Base will store the physical address of \"Page Table of the Segment\".\n    Bound will store number of pages in the segment.\n-> MSB <- Segment Bits <-> VPN Bits <-> Page Offset -> LSB\n-> Address Translation: Base[Segment Number] + sizeof(PTE) * VPN\n\n-> This still causes \"EXTERNAL FRAGMENTATION\" for storing \"page tables\", cause they vary in size\n-> If we have a large but sparsely used heap, we can still end up wasting a lot of page table space like we did in Flat Page Table.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 31: Segmentation with Paging\n\n-> Segmentation solves Internal Fragmentation, Paging solves external fragmentation. Combine Both.\n-> Each process has \"segment\" number of page tables. One per each segment.\n-> The base and bound registers will be there per each segment. Base will store the physical address of \"Page Table of the Segment\".\n    Bound will store number of pages in the segment.\n-> MSB <- Segment Bits <-> VPN Bits <-> Page Offset -> LSB\n-> Address Translation: Base[Segment Number] + sizeof(PTE) * VPN\n\n-> This still causes \"EXTERNAL FRAGMENTATION\" for storing \"page tables\", cause they vary in size\n-> If we have a large but sparsely used heap, we can still end up wasting a lot of page table space like we did in Flat Page Table.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "1hYsP712DvQ99TpTFCt1e",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 982.6796035534223,
      "y": 4823.76269449821,
      "width": 1490.558837890625,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b63",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 307090904,
      "version": 2561,
      "versionNonce": 1265251752,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781525020498,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lecture 32: Demand Paging and Page Replacement Algorithms\n\n-> Strictly Speaking, \"Virtual Memory\" refers to the technique of using more memory(VA > PA) than the physical memory available using Disk.\n-> This is possible because of a technique called Demand Paging.\n-> In this, if we run out of pages, we choose a page to be written into disk and use this page for serving the request. This is called Page Replacement.\n-> We make this technique more robust by making use of Dirty Bit and Present Bit and Referenced Bit.\n\nThe process:\n1) CPU wants to access some memory using a virtual address.\n2) First, we find out the page that it wants to access and look it up in the page table.\n3) The entry is there in the page table, but present bit is 0\n4) Now this means the page it wants to access is not in memory but is saved in the disk.\n5) This is called page fault and the hardware triggers a trap\n6) The OS takes over from here and few cases might happen.\n    Case 1: There is a free frame available\n    -> In this case the OS will just bring the frame onto the memory and updates the PTE and TLB\n\n    Case 2: No free frame Available. You must choose a \"victim\" page to be swapped onto disk.\n    -> Dirty Bit is 0 (This means the data inside this page is not modified, That means the copy of this page is already present in the disk)\n    -> Dirty Bit is 1 (This means the data inside this page is modified and it needs to be written onto disk before swapping with requested frame.)\n\n    -> Based on the dirty bit, the victim page might be written onto disk. The affected bits for this page are update in page tables and TLB.\n    -> Then the requested page is brought into main memory and the affected bits for this page are updated in Page tables and TLB.\n\n-> Pure Demand Paging: The OS won't allocate the physical memory unless for the page, unless it is addressed. The initial data of the process is \n    stored on the disk in form of pages and are brought into the main memory only when they are addressed.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 32: Demand Paging and Page Replacement Algorithms\n\n-> Strictly Speaking, \"Virtual Memory\" refers to the technique of using more memory(VA > PA) than the physical memory available using Disk.\n-> This is possible because of a technique called Demand Paging.\n-> In this, if we run out of pages, we choose a page to be written into disk and use this page for serving the request. This is called Page Replacement.\n-> We make this technique more robust by making use of Dirty Bit and Present Bit and Referenced Bit.\n\nThe process:\n1) CPU wants to access some memory using a virtual address.\n2) First, we find out the page that it wants to access and look it up in the page table.\n3) The entry is there in the page table, but present bit is 0\n4) Now this means the page it wants to access is not in memory but is saved in the disk.\n5) This is called page fault and the hardware triggers a trap\n6) The OS takes over from here and few cases might happen.\n    Case 1: There is a free frame available\n    -> In this case the OS will just bring the frame onto the memory and updates the PTE and TLB\n\n    Case 2: No free frame Available. You must choose a \"victim\" page to be swapped onto disk.\n    -> Dirty Bit is 0 (This means the data inside this page is not modified, That means the copy of this page is already present in the disk)\n    -> Dirty Bit is 1 (This means the data inside this page is modified and it needs to be written onto disk before swapping with requested frame.)\n\n    -> Based on the dirty bit, the victim page might be written onto disk. The affected bits for this page are update in page tables and TLB.\n    -> Then the requested page is brought into main memory and the affected bits for this page are updated in Page tables and TLB.\n\n-> Pure Demand Paging: The OS won't allocate the physical memory unless for the page, unless it is addressed. The initial data of the process is \n    stored on the disk in form of pages and are brought into the main memory only when they are addressed.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "yK9GyFn5Tu0DUiJorcTjK",
